{"meta":{"chapter":"02","section":"03","title":"Equilibrium of Rigid Bodies","course":"statics","learning_objectives":[{"id":"statics-02-03-lo-01","statement":"Apply equilibrium equations for rigid bodies"},{"id":"statics-02-03-lo-02","statement":"Write three equilibrium equations: ΣFx=0, ΣFy=0, ΣM=0"},{"id":"statics-02-03-lo-03","statement":"Solve statically determinate problems"},{"id":"statics-02-03-lo-04","statement":"Identify support reactions"}],"concept_ids":["rigid_body_equilibrium","support_reactions","beam_equilibrium"],"prerequisites":["moment","couple"],"prereq_section_ids":["statics/02/02","statics/02/01"],"practice_ids":["statics_rigid_body_support_count","statics_beam_center_load","statics_cantilever_moment","statics_choose_moment_point","statics_02_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":55,"lo_practice_map":{"statics-02-03-lo-01":["statics_choose_moment_point","statics_cantilever_moment"],"statics-02-03-lo-02":["statics_rigid_body_support_count"],"statics-02-03-lo-03":["statics_02_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"statics-02-03-lo-04":["statics_beam_center_load"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Equilibrium of Rigid Bodies\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Concept relationship map connecting Rigid Body Equilibrium, Support Reactions, and Beam Equilibrium within Equilibrium of Rigid Bodies.\" conceptid=\"rigid_body_equilibrium\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/reference-figures/statics/02-03-equilibrium-of-rigid-bodies.png\" alt=\"Equilibrium of Rigid Bodies concept map with Rigid Body Equilibrium, Support Reactions, and Beam Equilibrium connected to the section focus.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n> **A rigid body is in equilibrium only when both forces and moments balance.**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Balcony That Rotated\n\nIn June 2015, a fifth-floor apartment balcony in Berkeley, California sheared off its supports and collapsed, killing six people. The investigation traced the failure to rotted wooden cantilever joists: the connection at the wall could no longer supply the **reaction moment** needed to hold the balcony horizontal. The vertical loads were modest, well within what the floor could carry. What the building could no longer provide was the turning resistance at the support.\n\nThat distinction is the heart of this section. A particle only needs its forces to cancel. A rigid body, because it occupies space and can spin about any point, also needs its **moments** to cancel. Many real structural failures are not \"the load was too heavy\" failures; they are \"the support could not resist rotation\" failures. Cantilevered balconies, wall-mounted brackets, retaining walls that tip, and cranes that overturn all fail the moment equation long before they fail the force equation.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Rigid-body equilibrium is the first analytical screen in structural and machine design. It tells you the support reactions, where the load path concentrates, and whether a layout is even worth detailing further.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you should be able to:\n\n1. **Apply** the three planar equilibrium equations for rigid bodies,\n2. **Identify** support reactions for common support types,\n3. **Choose** moment points that eliminate unknowns,\n4. **Solve** statically determinate beam and frame problems, including distributed and inclined loads,\n5. **Assess** static determinacy by counting unknowns against available equations.\n\n---\n\n## From Particle Equilibrium to Rigid-Body Equilibrium\n\nFor a particle, force balance is enough:\n\n$$\n\\sum \\vec{F} = 0\n$$\n\nBut a rigid body can rotate, so force balance alone is not sufficient. Imagine two equal and opposite forces offset from each other, a **couple**. Their vector sum is exactly zero, yet they spin the body. Force balance cannot detect this, so we add a second condition.\n\nA rigid body in 2D must satisfy:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\sum F_x = 0,\\qquad \\sum F_y = 0,\\qquad \\sum M_O = 0}\n$$\n\nThese three equations capture two ideas:\n\n- the body must not translate in $x$ or $y$,\n- and it must not have a net turning tendency about any point.\n\nA subtle and useful fact: if the forces balance ($\\sum \\vec F = 0$) and the moment about **one** point is zero, then the moment about **every** point is zero. This is why we are free to pick the most convenient moment center. In 3D the same logic gives six scalar equations ($\\sum F_x=\\sum F_y=\\sum F_z=0$ and $\\sum M_x=\\sum M_y=\\sum M_z=0$).\n\n> **Key Insight:** A single moment equation can be written about any point in the plane, and a wise choice of that point can deliver an unknown directly without simultaneous equations.\n\n---\n\n## Support Reactions\n\nBefore writing equations, identify what each support can provide. A support can resist a motion only by supplying a reaction in that direction.\n\n| Support | Reactions Provided | Unknowns |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Roller / smooth surface | one force normal to the surface | 1 |\n| Pin / hinge | horizontal and vertical force components | 2 |\n| Fixed (built-in) | horizontal force, vertical force, reaction moment | 3 |\n| Cable / link | tension along its own line of action | 1 |\n\nThe support model determines the unknowns before the algebra even starts. A pin \"pins down\" translation in two directions but lets the body rotate, so it supplies two force components and no moment. A fixed support also prevents rotation, so it adds the reaction moment $M_A$.\n\n### Static Determinacy\n\nCount the unknown reaction components, call it $r$, and compare to the number of independent equilibrium equations, here $3$ in 2D:\n\n- $r < 3$: **unstable / mechanism** (cannot resist all loads),\n- $r = 3$: **statically determinate** (solvable by equilibrium alone),\n- $r > 3$: **statically indeterminate** (needs material deformation relations too).\n\nEvery worked example below is determinate ($r = 3$), which is exactly why equilibrium alone solves them.\n\n---\n\n## A Reliable Problem-Solving Strategy\n\nUse the same sequence every time:\n\n1. Draw the free-body diagram (FBD) of the whole body.\n2. Replace supports with their reaction forces and moments.\n3. Choose a coordinate system and a sign convention (counterclockwise positive is standard).\n4. Replace any distributed load with its resultant at the centroid of the load.\n5. Pick a smart moment point to eliminate as many unknowns as possible.\n6. Write the three equilibrium equations and solve.\n7. Check whether the reactions make physical sense.\n\nStudents often rush to equations before completing the FBD. That usually causes sign mistakes or missing reactions.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Draw the free-body diagram of the whole body\",\"Replace supports with their reaction forces and moments\",\"Choose a coordinate system and sign convention (CCW positive)\",\"Replace any distributed load with its resultant at the centroid\",\"Pick a smart moment point to eliminate unknowns\",\"Write the three equilibrium equations and solve\",\"Check whether the reactions make physical sense\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 1: Simply Supported Beam\n\n**Problem:** A 6 m beam is pinned at A and on a roller at B. A 1000 N downward load acts at midspan (3 m from A). Find the reactions.\n\n**FBD:** Pin A gives $A_x$ and $A_y$; roller B gives a vertical $B_y$; the 1000 N load points down at midspan. Unknowns $r = 3$, so the beam is determinate.\n\nTake moments about A so the pin reactions drop out (their lines of action pass through A):\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0:\\quad B_y(6) - 1000(3) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; B_y = 500 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nThen vertical balance:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0:\\quad A_y + 500 - 1000 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 500 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nAnd horizontal balance, with no horizontal applied load:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_x = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A_x = 0,\\quad A_y = 500\\text{ N},\\quad B_y = 500\\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThe symmetry check holds: equal reactions for a central load, each carrying half the total. Note how choosing A as the moment center turned a three-unknown system into a one-line solution.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Simply supported beam: reactions vs load position\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"P\",\"label\":\"Point load magnitude\",\"min\":0,\"max\":2000,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"N\",\"default\":1000},{\"name\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Load distance from pin A\",\"min\":0,\"max\":6,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"L\",\"label\":\"Span length A to B\",\"min\":1,\"max\":6,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":6}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Roller reaction By = P a / L\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"P * a / L\"},{\"label\":\"Pin vertical reaction Ay = P (L - a) / L\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"P * (L - a) / L\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"a\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSlide the load toward B and the roller reaction $B_y$ rises while $A_y$ falls; the support nearer the load always carries more. At midspan ($a = L/2$) they are equal, recovering the symmetric result above.\n\n<inline-check question=\"On this simply supported beam, you move the point load from midspan toward the roller at B. What happens to the pin reaction Ay at the far support?\" explanation=\"From Ay = P(L - a)/L, increasing a (load nearer B) shrinks (L - a), so Ay decreases while By increases. The support closer to the load takes the larger share.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Ay decreases\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Ay increases\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Ay stays at P/2 regardless of position\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Cantilever Beam\n\n**Problem:** A 4 m cantilever is fixed at A and carries a 2000 N downward load at the free end. Find the wall reactions.\n\n**FBD:** The fixed support supplies $A_x$, $A_y$, and a reaction moment $M_A$. Unknowns $r = 3$, determinate.\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_x = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y - 2000 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 2000 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nMoments about A (the load sits 4 m out, turning the beam clockwise, so the wall must supply a counterclockwise $M_A$):\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; M_A - 2000(4) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; M_A = 8000 \\text{ N\\,m}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A_x = 0,\\quad A_y = 2000\\text{ N},\\quad M_A = 8000\\text{ N\\,m}}\n$$\n\nThis is the Berkeley balcony lesson in algebra: the vertical force is easy to supply, but the 8000 N m reaction moment is what the support must not lose. A connection that cannot deliver $M_A$ rotates and fails.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Rigid Body Equilibrium\" context=\"A product engineer is checking whether a wall-mounted robotic arm bracket will overload its anchor bolts when a payload is extended outward from the wall.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Beam with a Distributed Load\n\n**Problem:** A 6 m simply supported beam (pin at A, roller at B) carries a uniformly distributed load of $w = 400$ N/m over its entire length. Find the reactions.\n\n**Step 1: Replace the distribution with its resultant.** A uniform load has a rectangular diagram, so the resultant is its area, acting at the centroid (the midpoint):\n\n$$\nR = w L = (400)(6) = 2400 \\text{ N at } x = 3 \\text{ m}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Moments about A.**\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0:\\quad B_y(6) - 2400(3) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; B_y = 1200 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Force balance.**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0:\\quad A_y + 1200 - 2400 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 1200 \\text{ N}, \\qquad A_x = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A_x = 0,\\quad A_y = 1200\\text{ N},\\quad B_y = 1200\\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThe total load splits evenly because the resultant lands at midspan. The key technique is to convert the distributed load into a single equivalent force **before** taking moments.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Inclined Cable Support (Pin plus Cable)\n\n**Problem:** A horizontal bracket arm is pinned at A and held by a cable from its tip to a wall above. The arm is 2 m long; a 500 N weight hangs at the tip. The cable makes $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal where it meets the arm at the tip. Find the cable tension $T$ and the pin reactions.\n\n**FBD:** Pin A gives $A_x$, $A_y$. The cable at the tip pulls back toward the wall: its components are $T\\cos 30^\\circ$ (horizontal, toward A) and $T\\sin 30^\\circ$ (vertical, up). The 500 N weight pulls down at the tip. Unknowns $r = 3$, determinate.\n\n**Step 1: Moments about A** eliminate the pin reactions. Only the vertical forces at the tip (2 m out) create moment:\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0:\\quad T\\sin 30^\\circ (2) - 500(2) = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT(0.5)(2) = 1000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; T = \\frac{1000}{1.0} = 1000 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T = 1000 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Horizontal balance.** The cable pulls the tip toward the wall, so its horizontal component is directed back at A; the pin must push outward:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0:\\quad A_x - T\\cos 30^\\circ = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_x = 1000(0.866) = 866 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Vertical balance.**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0:\\quad A_y + T\\sin 30^\\circ - 500 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 500 - 1000(0.5) = 0 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A_x = 866 \\text{ N},\\quad A_y = 0}\n$$\n\nThe shallow cable angle forces a large tension (twice the hung weight) and a large horizontal pin thrust. This is the same lesson as shallow guy wires: low angles multiply internal force. A steeper cable would cut both $T$ and $A_x$ sharply.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Solve the inclined-cable bracket step by step (Example 4)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"A 2 m arm is pinned at A; a 500 N weight hangs at the tip; a cable at the tip pulls at 30 deg above horizontal. Which moment point kills BOTH pin unknowns at once?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The tip of the arm\",\"Point A (the pin)\",\"The midpoint of the arm\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Take moments about A: the pin reactions Ax and Ay pass through A, so they drop out, leaving one equation in T.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Only the vertical forces at the tip (2 m out) create a moment about A. Solve T sin(30)*(2) - 500*(2) = 0 for T.\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"T = 500 N\",\"T = 866 N\",\"T = 1000 N\",\"T = 250 N\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"T(0.5)(2) = 1000, so T = 1000 N. The shallow 30 deg angle makes the tension twice the hung weight.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Now horizontal balance: the cable pulls the tip toward the wall with T cos(30). What is Ax?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Ax = 0\",\"Ax = 500 N\",\"Ax = 866 N\",\"Ax = 1000 N\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Ax = T cos(30) = 1000 * 0.866 = 866 N. The pin must push outward to balance the cable pull.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Vertical balance: Ay + T sin(30) - 500 = 0. What is Ay?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Ay = 0\",\"Ay = 500 N\",\"Ay = 1000 N\",\"Ay = 866 N\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Ay = 500 - 1000(0.5) = 0. The cable carries the full vertical load, so the pin supplies only horizontal thrust.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final: T = 1000 N, Ax = 866 N, Ay = 0. Choosing A as the moment center reduced a three-unknown system to a single equation for T, then force balance gave the pin reactions directly.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\nWhen rigid-body equilibrium problems go wrong, it is usually because of one of these issues:\n\n- forgetting the reaction moment at a fixed support,\n- using the wrong sign convention for moments,\n- missing a reaction component at a pin,\n- using nonperpendicular distances as moment arms,\n- or leaving a distributed load as is instead of replacing it with its resultant.\n\nA useful self-check:\n\n- Are all support reactions included, and does the count give $r = 3$?\n- Did I use perpendicular distances in moment calculations?\n- Does the final answer agree with the likely direction of support action?\n\n---\n\n## Design Interpretation\n\nIn practice, rigid-body equilibrium is often the first screen for whether a concept is even worth detailing further.\n\nFor example, if a bracket layout produces a very large reaction moment at one support, that may signal:\n\n- a need to move the load path closer to the wall,\n- a need to spread load through two supports instead of one,\n- or a need to redesign the connection rather than simply thicken the member.\n\nThis is why reaction calculations matter beyond homework. They tell you where anchors, welds, bolts, and bearing surfaces are likely to become critical.\n\n### Common Modeling Traps\n\nEven when the equations are correct, engineers can still misuse the model. Watch for:\n\n- assuming a support behaves like a pin when it actually has partial fixity,\n- ignoring the eccentricity of a real load path,\n- treating a distributed load as a point load without checking where the resultant acts,\n- and trusting symmetry when the geometry or loading is only approximately symmetric.\n\nEquilibrium is a powerful first model, but it remains a model. Better design decisions come from knowing both what the model captures and what it leaves out.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Why Rigid-Body Equilibrium Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cantilever balconies and signage | Support must supply the reaction moment, not just vertical force |\n| Tower-crane jibs | Counterweight moment must balance the lifted-load moment about the mast |\n| Retaining walls | Overturning moment from soil must be resisted to avoid tipping |\n| Wall-mounted equipment brackets | Anchor bolts sized from the reaction moment and shear |\n| Bridge girders | Pin/roller reactions set the internal shear and bending |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** A crane that lifts a heavy load near its base may be perfectly safe, while the same load far out on the jib overturns it. The force never changed; the moment did.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Planar equilibrium | $\\sum F_x = 0,\\;\\sum F_y = 0,\\;\\sum M_O = 0$ |\n| Moment of a force | $M = F \\, d_\\perp$ |\n| Uniform load resultant | $R = wL$ at the load centroid |\n| Static determinacy (2D) | determinate when $r = 3$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Force balance is necessary but not sufficient for a rigid body; moments must balance too.\n- A moment equation can be taken about any point, so choose the point that removes the most unknowns.\n- Each support type supplies a known set of reactions; identify them before solving.\n- Replace distributed loads with their resultant at the centroid before taking moments.\n- Count unknowns: $r = 3$ in 2D means equilibrium alone will solve the problem.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section we move from finding reactions to finding the **internal** forces they create: shear and bending moment along a beam. The reactions computed here become the starting boundary values for those internal-force diagrams.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"rigid_body_equilibrium\",\"support_reactions\",\"beam_equilibrium\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why can a beam with zero net horizontal and vertical force still fail to be in equilibrium?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because moments may still produce a net tendency to rotate the beam.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because force balance automatically guarantees moment balance.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because beams cannot be analyzed with free-body diagrams.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. A rigid body can have balanced forces but still rotate if the net moment is not zero.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"statics_rigid_body_support_count","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"support_reactions","concept_ids":["support_reactions","rigid_body_equilibrium"],"stem":"Which 2D support provides three reaction unknowns?","options":["Roller","Cable","Pin","Fixed support"],"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A fixed support provides horizontal and vertical reactions plus a reaction moment.","misconception_id":"fixed_support_no_moment","learning_objective_ids":["statics-02-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"statics_beam_center_load","type":"numeric","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"beam_equilibrium","concept_ids":["beam_equilibrium","support_reactions"],"stem":"A simply supported 6 m beam carries a 1000 N point load at midspan. Find the vertical reaction at each support.","final_answer":{"value":500,"unit":"N","tolerance":0.5},"learning_objective_ids":["statics-02-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_cantilever_moment","type":"numeric","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"rigid_body_equilibrium","concept_ids":["rigid_body_equilibrium","support_reactions"],"stem":"A 4 m cantilever carries a 2000 N downward load at the free end. Find the reaction moment at the fixed support.","final_answer":{"value":8000,"unit":"N m","tolerance":1},"learning_objective_ids":["statics-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"statics_choose_moment_point","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"rigid_body_equilibrium","concept_ids":["rigid_body_equilibrium","support_reactions"],"stem":"Why is it often smart to take moments about a support point with unknown reactions?","options":["Because moment equations work only at supports","Because unknown reactions through that point create zero moment arm and drop out","Because support points always have zero load","Because it avoids the need for force equilibrium equations"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Choosing a point where unknown reaction lines pass through can eliminate those unknowns from the moment equation.","misconception_id":"force_balance_enough","learning_objective_ids":["statics-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"statics_02_03_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Solve statically determinate problems.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Solve statically determinate problems, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["statics-02-03-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"force_balance_enough","pattern":"rigid_body_error","description":"Assuming rigid-body equilibrium only requires force balance","trigger":"learner sets sum Fx and sum Fy to zero but omits the moment equation","feedback":"A rigid body can rotate even when the net force is zero. In 2D statics, you need sum Fx = 0, sum Fy = 0, and sum M = 0.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"fixed_support_no_moment","pattern":"support_model_error","description":"Forgetting that a fixed support resists rotation","trigger":"learner models a cantilever support with force reactions only","feedback":"A fixed support in 2D provides two force components and a reaction moment. Omitting the reaction moment makes the support model incomplete.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"moment_arm_wrong_distance","pattern":"geometry_error","description":"Using a nonperpendicular distance in the moment term","trigger":"learner multiplies force by an arbitrary slanted distance","feedback":"Moments use the perpendicular distance from the reference point to the line of action of the force, not just any measured length.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"6a5ae8c39506c2fe2d962eae8895a0df07b83d65e0882b57c74d873218f52a89","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}